 1 big thing: America&#x27;s meetup explosion 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Young people raised on swiping, DMing and streaming are doing something bold: going out, Axios' Carly Mallenbaum writes .

 Why it matters: Amid a loneliness epidemic , millennials and Gen Zers are craving real-life connection — enough to show up for meetups around activities once considered solitary or " grandma hobbies ." 🦜 The latest: They're leaving the house to play chess , birdwatch and read in silence together.

 "It's very easy to sit inside and rot just by yourself," Cole Robinson, president of the Audubon on Campus Trinity University chapter, tells Axios San Antonio . But activities like birding can help you "feel a little less lonely," the 20-year-old says. "Soft socializing" experiences — where you're with other people but networking isn't the focus — have taken off, Eventbrite found .

 Data : Eventbrite. (U.S. Eventbrite data for in-person, publicly listed events that include connection-oriented language in their descriptions.) Chart: Brad Jennings/Axios 💗 The trend extends to finding love, too. Americans are seeking romantic connection at so-called " meet-cute " events — settings meant to spark a sweet "How'd you meet?" story, away from apps.

 The meetups can revolve around trivia, PowerPoint presentations , crafts and even exercise . Dinner parties with strangers have gained traction among singles.

 Axios just launched our own dinner series, bringing readers together through the new Axios Social Club starting in Charlotte, Chicago, Kansas City and San Antonio. "Everybody's been stuck behind their phones for the past two decades, and it's not good for humanity," writer Stuart Schuffman — who recently launched an IRL dinner club for his Broke-Ass Stuart readers — tells Axios San Francisco. 

 People "want more connection and less doomscrolling ," he says. Share this story ... Subscribe to Axios Local , now in 41 cities. 

 
 
