 3. ➡️ AOC&#x27;s shift to the center 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Photo: Dia Dipasupil/Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Rep. Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez (D-N.Y.) is laughing off " Woke 1 ." But she's carrying baggage from the 2020 era of cultural progressivism that some Democrats say could complicate a future presidential run.

 Why it matters: AOC's pivot to the cultural center is potential damage control for past remarks about law enforcement, immigration and a range of social issues, Axios' Holly Otterbein and Alex Thompson report. "This is really going to be something that handicaps her, especially with demographics that are culturally more moderate," former Biden White House adviser Yemisi Egbewole tells Axios, pointing to Black voters in the South, who have a large voice in Democratic presidential primaries.

 Flashback: In the early 2020s, Ocasio-Cortez was one of the most aggressive and well-known advocates for "defunding" the police — a slogan that polls have long shown is unpopular.

 On immigration, Ocasio-Cortez cosponsored a bill in 2021 and 2023 to decriminalize border crossings. She said in 2019 that some marginalized groups "have no choice but to riot." Zoom out: AOC has shifted her rhetoric but hasn't detailed where she currently stands on many of these politically fraught topics, or what made her change her views on "Woke 1."

 Ocasio-Cortez's spokespeople didn't respond to several questions, including her current position on defunding police, whether she supports decriminalizing border crossings, and if she thinks rioting is sometimes justified. Democratic strategist James Carville praised AOC, saying "it's always been evident that she's more talented than the rest of them put together," referring to the "Squad" of House progressives.

 Carville added that AOC doesn't need to over-explain her changes of heart: "She can say: 'I'm grown,' and people understand that." Share this story . 

 
 
