 7. 🐥 Flock freakout 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Activists and citizens across the political spectrum are pushing back against license plate readers made by Flock and similar companies, fueled by privacy concerns and suspected police misuse.

 Why it matters: Flock cameras are suddenly emerging as the next big target of populist anti-tech, pro-privacy sentiment, alongside the data centers fueling the AI boom, Axios' Astrid Galván reports. The big picture: Flock has over 120,000 cameras across the country, the company tells Axios.

 Advocates say the cameras help quickly track down criminals, making cities safer. Opponents say they're a form of constant surveillance that infringes on residents' civil liberties. Some have even turned to sabotaging the cameras . The latest: Flock said last week it's taking steps to improve privacy, including cutting some default data retention settings from 30 days to 7 days.

